        "skip-intro": "Skip",
        "southwest-caption": "Cape Marengo &mdash; spelt &ldquo;Maringo&rdquo; &mdash; and Cape des Représentations, a name assigned by Baudin and later replaced by Péron and Freycinet with Cape Suffren. The latter name was added in pencil above &ldquo;Représentations&rdquo; on the original document. The location is now known as Point Grey.<br>30 March 1802.<br>Wash drawings of the coasts of the <em>Terres Australes</em> by Charles-Alexandre Lesueur.",
        "southwest-source": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.siv.archives-nationales.culture.gouv.fr/siv/media/FRAN_IR_054071/c2vb0ou1jo98--clf15o88p7oi/FRAN_0044_02228_L\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Archives nationales de France</a>.",
        "southwest-title": "New Holland, South-West Coast, Coastal Profile",
        "supporters-bellec-bio": "<strong><a href=\"https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fran%C3%A7ois_Bellec\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">François Bellec</a></strong>, rear admiral, author, painter, and former director of the Musée national de la Marine. As a member of the Académie de Marine, he has advanced the appreciation of French maritime explorations and their cartographic heritage.",
        "supporters-bellec-title": "Message from Rear Admiral François Bellec",
        "supporters-bellec-work": "He produced twelve video films on maritime history and wrote around twenty books, along with many contributions to collective and encyclopedic works. As a historian of navigation, great voyages, and maritime art, his writings have been translated into German, English, and Portuguese. A sought-after speaker, he has presented on the sea and art in France and abroad (United States, Russia, China, Thailand, Indonesia, Australia).",
        "supporters-hay-bio": "<strong><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iain_Hay\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Iain Hay</a></strong> is Distinguished Professor Emeritus of Geography at Flinders University and inaugural Director of the Royal Geographical Society of South Australia. He has held leadership positions at institutional, state, national and international levels, including as: First Vice President of the International Geographical Union (IGU); President of the Institute of Australian Geographers (IAG); Australian Learning and Teaching Council Discipline Scholar for the Humanities, Arts and Social Sciences; Chair of the Australian Academy of Science’s National Committee for Geographical Sciences; and foundation Dean (Education) of the College of Humanities, Arts and Social Sciences at Flinders University.",
        "supporters-hay-text": "Click on the title or the photo to read the full text.",
        "supporters-hay-title": "A word from Iain Hay",
        "supporters-meta-title": "Project Supporters — French Place Names Australia",
        "supporters-rgssa-text": "<strong>Acknowledgements</strong><br>The development of this website was made possible through the generous support of the Royal Geographical Society of South Australia (RGSSA).",
        "supporters-rgssa-text-2": "I would like to express my sincere gratitude to the Director, Iain Hay, and the Vice President, Guy Robinson, for their encouragement, vision, and commitment. Their initiative played a decisive role in bringing this project to fruition, and their sustained interest provided constant motivation throughout its development.",
        "supporters-rgssa-text-3": "<strong>Resources</strong><br>The RGSSA library preserves and provides access to original editions of several major works relating to French expeditions in the southern seas.<br>Among these resources are, for the d’Entrecasteaux expedition, the Atlas of the Voyage of Bruny d’Entrecasteaux (1808) and Labillardière’s Voyage in Search of La Pérouse (Vol. 1, English translation, 1800); and for the Baudin expedition and its nomenclature, Péron’s account of the voyage, with maps by Freycinet (1807), Freycinet’s Voyage of Discoveries to the Southern Lands… Navigation and Geography (1815), as well as earlier works that informed geographical knowledge of the time, such as Sonnerat’s Voyage to New Guinea (Paris, 1776) and Buffon’s Natural History, General and Particular (illustrated edition, 1785).",
        "supporters-rgssa-title": "The Royal Geographical Society of South Australia",
        "supporters-robinson-bio": "<strong><a href=\"https://researchers.adelaide.edu.au/profile/guy.robinson\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Guy M Robinson</a></strong> is an Emeritus Professor in the Department of Geography, Environment and Population at Adelaide University and a Fellow of the Laboratory for Interdisciplinary Spatial Analysis (LISA) in the Department of Land Economy at the University of Cambridge, UK. He is Editor-in-Chief of <em>Research in Globalization</em> and the author of 16 books, including <em>Transforming Rural China</em> (2024), <em>Handbook on the Globalisation of Agriculture</em> (2nd edition 2027) and <em>Sustainable Rural Systems</em> (2008). The author of &gt;150 papers in refereed journals and 90 book chapters, his research focuses on rural development and environmental management. He is a Fellow of the Institute of Australian Geographers (IAG) (awarded July 2025) and was Editor-in-Chief of <em>Land Use Policy</em> for 12 years.",
        "supporters-robinson-title": "Message from Guy Robinson",
        "supporters-wahf-text": "I would like to express my sincere thanks to the Western Australian History Foundation, whose generous grant has greatly assisted in the development of this website project, providing especially welcome support in light of the challenges encountered in securing funding.",
        "supporters-wahf-title": "The Western Australian History Foundation",
